## Requirements<a id="requirements"></a>

Install [Molecule](https://docs.ansible.com/projects/molecule/installation/).
The scenarios use Molecule's `default` driver with their own create and destroy
playbooks, so they need no Molecule driver plugin. Before each run, Molecule
installs the collections a scenario needs from `<scenario>/requirements.yml`.



### Scenario `default`<a id="requirements-scenario-default"></a>

Each platform in `default/molecule.yml` selects its backend with the `type` key:
`"podman"` for a container, which is also the default without a `type` key, or
`"libvirt"` for a QEMU/KVM virtual machine. Install what the listed platforms
need.

Container platforms need [Podman](https://podman.io/docs/installation). Set
`MOLECULE_PODMAN_EXECUTABLE` to use another Podman binary than `podman`.

Virtual machine platforms need:

1. libvirt with the QEMU driver and client, the QEMU tools and
   [passt](https://passt.top/):

   ```bash
   # Fedora
   sudo dnf install libvirt-daemon-driver-qemu libvirt-client qemu-kvm qemu-img passt

   # Debian, Ubuntu
   sudo apt install libvirt-daemon libvirt-clients qemu-system-x86 qemu-utils passt
   ```

   The scenario uses the libvirt user session (`qemu:///session`), which needs
   no root privileges and no membership in the `libvirt` group.
2. Access to `/dev/kvm`. Users logged in locally on Fedora, Debian and Ubuntu
   have it by default. Headless sessions on Debian and Ubuntu, such as SSH
   sessions or CI runners, also need membership in the `kvm` group
   (`sudo usermod -aG kvm <user>`, then log in again).
3. The [`pycdlib`](https://pypi.org/project/pycdlib/) Python package in
   Molecule's Python environment. It builds the cloud-init NoCloud seed ISO
   images:

   ```bash
   pip install "pycdlib"

   # or with uv
   uv pip install "pycdlib"
   ```



### Scenario `ee`<a id="requirements-scenario-ee"></a>

The `ee` scenario needs [Podman](https://podman.io/docs/installation) and
`ansible-builder`:

```bash
pip install "ansible-builder"

# or with uv
uv pip install "ansible-builder"
```



## Usage<a id="usage"></a>

Run Molecule from the project's root directory, with `MOLECULE_GLOB` matching
both the role layout (`molecule/`) and the collection layout
(`extensions/molecule/`). The examples below assume these two commands. The
[FAQ](#faq-role-path-detection) explains why the variable is set.

```bash
cd "/path/to/project"
export MOLECULE_GLOB="./{extensions/molecule,molecule}/*/molecule.yml"
```



### Run tests<a id="usage-testing"></a>

{# skeleton-variant: nox #}
#### Through nox<a id="usage-nox"></a>

`nox -s molecule` runs the `default` scenario. It prepares the collection and
the scenario requirements in a temporary tree, so it installs nothing into
`~/.ansible`. All platforms run in one Molecule run, unless the platform list
contains a virtual machine: then each platform runs on its own, one after the
other. `MOLECULE_SERIAL=true` or `MOLECULE_SERIAL=false` overrides that choice.

Arguments after `--` go to Molecule, so the commands in the following sections
also work as `nox -s molecule -- <command>`. Molecule takes one platform name
per run, on `test` only. See [`DEVELOPMENT.md`](../../DEVELOPMENT.md) for the
other checks.

{# end skeleton-variant #}
#### Scenario `default`<a id="usage-testing-default"></a>

```bash
# Test all platforms listed in default/molecule.yml
molecule test

# Test one platform with more Ansible output
molecule -vvvv test --platform-name="molecule-debian12"

# Keep the instances after testing, then destroy them when done
molecule test --destroy="never"
molecule test --platform-name="molecule-debian12" --destroy="never"
molecule list
molecule destroy
```

To switch a platform between container and virtual machine, swap its active
entry in `default/molecule.yml`. The comments there list the keys of each
backend. One run can mix containers and virtual machines.



#### VM platforms (`type: "libvirt"`)<a id="usage-testing-vm"></a>

Use virtual machines for tests that containers cannot cover, such as kernel
settings, nested virtualization or Podman inside the test target. They boot
vendor cloud images in the libvirt user session with passt user networking,
which needs no root privileges, no `libvirt` group membership and no polkit
rules.

Virtual machines need far more resources than containers. On smaller machines,
test one platform at a time with `--platform-name`. A resource guard stops the
`create` step early when the requested memory plus a host reserve exceeds the
available memory, or when the image cache runs low on disk space.

The first run downloads each cloud image (qcow2, several hundred MiB) into the
image cache. Deleting the cache is safe; the scenario downloads missing images
again.

|            Variable            |                  Default                   | Purpose |
| ------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------ | ------- |
| `MOLECULE_VM_LIBVIRT_URI`      | `qemu:///session`                          | libvirt connection URI |
| `MOLECULE_VM_CACHE_DIR`        | `~/.cache/ansible-molecule-libvirt/images` | Cloud image cache directory |
| `MOLECULE_VM_REFRESH_IMAGES`   | (unset)                                    | Set to `1` to download the cached cloud images again |
| `MOLECULE_VM_IGNORE_RESOURCES` | (unset)                                    | Set to `1` to skip the memory and disk space guard |

If an instance does not come up:

- Read its boot console log, `vm/<name>/console.log` in Molecule's ephemeral
  directory (usually `~/.ansible/tmp/molecule.*.default/`).
- List the domain states: `virsh --connect 'qemu:///session' list --all`
- Ask the guest agent for the IP address:
  `virsh --connect 'qemu:///session' domifaddr '<name>' --source agent`
- Watch the boot output of each instance in
  [`virt-manager`](https://virt-manager.org/), connected to `qemu:///session`.



#### Scenario `ee`<a id="usage-testing-ee"></a>

The scenario builds the project artifact and an
[execution environment](https://docs.ansible.com/projects/ansible/latest/getting_started_ee/index.html)
image, then checks that the project is included in the EE:

```bash
molecule test --scenario-name "ee"
```



### Access instance terminal<a id="usage-instance-access"></a>

`molecule login` opens a shell in a running instance, for example one kept with
`--destroy="never"`. It works for containers and virtual machines:

```bash
molecule list
molecule login --host "<name>"
```



### Write tests<a id="usage-add-tests"></a>

The playbooks in `shared/playbooks/` run each Molecule phase. Every phase also
includes the task files of a directory under `<scenario>/tasks/`, in
alphabetical order:

|      Directory       |            Task files            | Use |
| -------------------- | -------------------------------- | --- |
| `tasks/converge/`    | `*.yml`                          | Apply the project. Failing tasks already fail the test. |
| `tasks/verify/`      | `*.yml`                          | Check the resulting state and behavior, usually with `ansible.builtin.assert`. |
| `tasks/prepare/`     | `all.yml`, `<instance name>.yml` | Configure all instances, or one, before converge. |
| `tasks/side_effect/` | `*.yml`                          | Change the instances between two test steps. |
| `tasks/cleanup/`     | `*.yml`                          | Remove what the tests left behind. |

The `*.yml.example` files in `default/tasks/` show how to apply the project.
Rename one to `*.yml` to use it.

Project-specific tests stay in these directories. You can therefore update the
rest of this directory from a newer rendered skeleton of
[foundata/ansible-skeletons](https://github.com/foundata/ansible-skeletons)
without losing them.



## FAQ, troubleshooting<a id="faq"></a>

### Molecule does not find the scenarios<a id="faq-role-path-detection"></a>

If Molecule does not find the roles or does not install the current version of
the project on each run, set `MOLECULE_GLOB` and run `molecule` from the
project's root directory instead of the `extensions` subdirectory:

```bash
cd "/path/to/project"
export MOLECULE_GLOB="./{extensions/molecule,molecule}/*/molecule.yml"
molecule list

# or for one command only
MOLECULE_GLOB="./{extensions/molecule,molecule}/*/molecule.yml" molecule list
```

Without the variable, Molecule looks for `molecule/*/molecule.yml` in the
working directory. Recent releases use `extensions/molecule/*/molecule.yml`
instead when the working directory contains a valid `galaxy.yml`. Further
reading:

- Source:
  [`molecule/command/base.py`](https://github.com/ansible/molecule/blob/main/src/molecule/command/base.py)
- Issues: [4015](https://github.com/ansible/molecule/issues/4015),
  [4017](https://github.com/ansible/molecule/issues/4017),
  [4040](https://github.com/ansible/molecule/issues/4040),
  [4061](https://github.com/ansible/molecule/issues/4061)
- Pull requests: [4177](https://github.com/ansible/molecule/pull/4177)



### Air-gapped environments<a id="faq-air-gapped"></a>

The scenarios download container images and the EE base image from
[quay.io](https://quay.io/), collections from Ansible Galaxy, cloud images for
virtual machines from the distributions, and packages from the distributions'
repositories. If your network provides mirrors:

1. Point `platforms[].image` in `<scenario>/molecule.yml` to your registry.
2. Configure the instances in `<scenario>/tasks/prepare/all.yml`, for example to
   use internal package mirrors:

   ```yaml
   ---
   - name: "Molecule | Prepare | Configure internal package repository mirrors for Debian/Ubuntu"
     when:
       - (ansible_facts['os_family'] | ansible.builtin.lower) == 'debian'
     block:
       - name: "Molecule | Prepare | Create sources.list"
         ansible.builtin.copy:
           dest: "/etc/apt/sources.list"
           content: |
             # Internal mirror for {{ '{{' }} ansible_facts['distribution'] {{ '}}' }} {{ '{{' }} ansible_facts['distribution_release'] {{ '}}' }}
             deb http://internal-mirror.example.com/debian {{ '{{' }} ansible_facts['distribution_release'] {{ '}}' }} main contrib non-free
             deb http://internal-mirror.example.com/debian {{ '{{' }} ansible_facts['distribution_release'] {{ '}}' }}-updates main contrib non-free
             deb http://internal-mirror.example.com/debian-security {{ '{{' }} ansible_facts['distribution_release'] {{ '}}' }}-security main contrib non-free

       - name: "Molecule | Prepare | Update apt cache"
         ansible.builtin.apt:
           update_cache: true
   ```

   To configure a single instance, put its tasks into `<instance name>.yml`
   instead of using `when:` conditions in `all.yml`.



### Container: `inotify_init1(): Too many open files`<a id="faq-inotify"></a>

If not all platform instances start in parallel, start fewer containers at once
with `--platform-name`, or raise the inotify limits:

```bash
# Inspect
sysctl fs.inotify

# Test higher values until the next reboot (example: 512, 524288)
echo 512 | sudo tee /proc/sys/fs/inotify/max_user_instances
echo 524288 | sudo tee /proc/sys/fs/inotify/max_user_watches

# Keep them across reboots
echo "fs.inotify.max_user_instances=512" | sudo tee -a /etc/sysctl.conf
echo "fs.inotify.max_user_watches=524288" | sudo tee -a /etc/sysctl.conf
sudo sysctl -p
```

To debug, keep the instances, find the stopped container and start it with
debug logging. Keep the complete output of the failing command:

```bash
# Reproduce the resource limit
molecule test --destroy="never"

# Inspect
podman images
podman ps --all --no-trunc
podman --log-level debug start <container-id>
```



### VMs: Instance-to-instance networking<a id="faq-vm-multi-node"></a>

Virtual machine instances cannot reach each other. [passt](https://passt.top/)
gives every instance its own isolated user-space network stack, and the host
reaches the instances through SSH port forwards. The shared playbooks do not
implement or support traffic between instances.

A scenario that needs it would have to use bridged networking through the
setuid `qemu-bridge-helper`
(`<interface type='bridge'><source bridge='virbr0'/>`). That confines the
privilege to one narrow, audited helper binary instead of making the whole user
session equivalent to root:

- **Fedora** ships everything needed (`/etc/qemu/bridge.conf` containing
  `allow virbr0`, setuid helper). One-time setup:
  `sudo virsh --connect 'qemu:///system' net-start default && sudo virsh --connect 'qemu:///system' net-autostart default`.
- **Debian and Ubuntu** deliberately ship the helper without setuid and without
  a `bridge.conf`. You would need
  `sudo dpkg-statoverride --update --add root root 4755 /usr/lib/qemu/qemu-bridge-helper`
  and to create `/etc/qemu/bridge.conf` with the content `allow virbr0`
  yourself. This changes the system's security posture; consider it carefully.
